    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d676">

                <pb facs="0615" n="606" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg606"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851.30/94: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Cole</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-10-29T15:00:00+01:00">October 29, 1940—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 30—2 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>139. Reliable source states, “Local high command of naval forces has received a
                    telegram from Admiral Darlan,<anchor n="79" xml:id="d676-f1"/> ‘I need French
                    Fleet kept in complete readiness day and night to save our Empire.’”</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cole</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d676-f1" n="79" xml:id="d676-f1-content">
                <p>Jean François Darlan, Minister of Marine in the French (Vichy) Government.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
